Title: Thomas Jefferson College
Order: 7
Production Code: 1x07

FADE IN:

EXT. WACO, TEXAS - RAILWAY STATION - DAY

[[Belton Piedmont]], apprised by telegram of the hour, is at the station to meet [[Bernard Belgrave]] after a journey of four days. He is shocked at the haggard appearance of his old play-fellow, such a contrast to the brilliant, glowing, handsome Bernard of former days.

After greetings they enter a carriage and drive through the city and out of it.

EXT. TEXAS ROAD - DAY

After about five miles they come in sight of a high stone wall enclosure of about twenty acres, with trees in patches. In the middle, upon a slight elevation, a building four stories high, about two hundred feet long and one hundred and eighty wide. In the center of the front a round tower, half of it bulging out, from the ground to about twenty feet above the roof. The entrance is a wide door in the tower. A few paces off, a small white cottage.

The carriage passes the tower door on a wide gravel driveway curving to another gate.

INSERT - CARVED STEPS

Carved in large letters on top of the stone steps: "Thomas Jefferson College."

INT. TOWER - ELEVATOR - DAY

They enter and find themselves on the floor of an elevator. They ascend to the fourth story.

INT. FOURTH FLOOR - [[Jefferson College]] - DAY

The whole story is one huge room, devoid of furniture save a table and two chairs in a corner. In the center, an elevated platform about ten feet square, on it what might pass for either a gallows or an acting pole.

Belton leads Bernard to the table. They sit. Belton informs him he brought him here so that there would be no possibility of anyone hearing.

BELTON
I have been so fortunate as to unearth a foul conspiracy that is being hatched by our people. I have decided to expose them and see every one of them hung.

BERNARD
Pray tell me, Belton, what is the motive that prompts you to be so zealous in the work of ferreting out conspirators among your people to be hanged by the whites?

BELTON
It is this: you know as it is, the Negro has a hard time in this country. If we begin to develop traitors and conspirators we shall fare even worse. It is necessary, therefore, that we kill these vipers that come, lest we all be slain as vipers. I desire to secure you as prosecuting attorney in the case. When we thus expose the traitors, we shall earn the gratitude of the government and our race will be treated with more consideration in the future.

BERNARD
Belton, I tell you frankly that my share in that kind of business will be infinitessimally small. But go on. Let me know the whole story, that I may know better what to think and do.

BELTON
You know that there is one serious flaw in the Constitution of the United States, which has already caused a world of trouble. You know that a ship's boilers, engines, rigging, and so forth may be in perfect condition, but a serious leak in her bottom will sink the proudest vessel afloat. This flaw is the relation of the General Government to the individual state. The General Government says to the citizen: "I am your sovereign. If I call on you to defend my sovereignty, you must do so even if you have to fight against your own state. But while I am your supreme earthly sovereign I am powerless to protect you against crimes, injustices, outrages against you. Your state may disfranchise you with or without law, may mob you; but my hands are so tied that I can't help you at all. If you are beset by Klu Klux, White Cappers, Bulldozers, Lynchers, do not turn your dying eyes on me for I am unable to help you."

BELTON (CONT'D)
The weakness of the General Government was revealed with startling clearness in connection with the murder of those Italians in New Orleans, a few years ago. It had promised Italy to afford protection to her citizens. When these men were murdered it could not even bring the murderers to trial. It had to confess its impotence to the world, and paid Italy a specified sum of money. The Negro finds himself an unprotected foreigner in his own home. So this conspiracy has been formed to unite all Negroes in a body to do that which the whimpering government childishly but truthfully says it cannot do. These men take a solemn oath, offering their very blood for the cause. I see that this will lead eventually to a clash of arms, and I wish to expose the conspiracy before it is too late. Cooperate with me and glory and honor shall attend us all of our days. Now, Bernard, tell me candidly what you think of the whole matter. May I not rely on you?

Bernard rises, pointing his finger at Belton.

BERNARD
Well, let me tell you just exactly what I think and just what I shall do. I think, sir, that you are the most infernal scoundrel that I ever saw, and those whom you call conspirators are a set of sublime patriots; and further, if you betray those men, I will kill you.

Belton does not seem enraged. He looks indifferent and unconcerned, and rises as if to yawn. Suddenly he throws himself on Bernard with the agility of a tiger and knocks him to the floor. From secret closets in the room spring six able bodied men. They soon have Bernard securely bound.

BELTON (CONT'D)
You must retract what you have said and agree to keep your revealed purpose a secret, or you shall never leave this room alive.

BERNARD
Then I shall die, and my only regret will be that I shall die at the hands of such an abominable wretch as you are.

Bernard is stood against the wall. The six men retire to their closets and return with rifles. They form a line ten paces in front of him. Belton gives Bernard one last chance, as he says, to save his life by silence as to his plans.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
If I live I shall surely proclaim your infamy to our people and slay you besides. The curse of our doomed race is just such white folks' niggers as you are. Shoot, shoot, shoot, you whelps.

They take aim and, at a command from Belton, fire. The smoke lifts. Bernard stands.

BELTON
Bernard, those were blank cartridges. I desired to give you another chance. If you consent to leave me unmolested to ferret out those conspirators I will take your word as your bond and spare your life. Will you accept your life at such a low price?

BERNARD
Come here and let me give you my answer. Let me whisper something in your ear.

Belton draws near. Bernard spits in his face.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
Take that, you knave.

Belton orders Bernard seized and carried to the center of the room, to what appears an acting pole but is a complete gallows. A black cap is adjusted over Bernard's head and a rope tied to his hands. He is told a horrible death awaits him: the platform is a trap door concealing an opening in the center of the building that extends to the first floor; he will be dropped far enough to have his arms torn from his body, and be left to die.

Bernard perceptibly shudders. He has determined long since to be true to every higher aspiration of his people. He mutters a prayer to God, closes his eyes, grits his teeth.

Belton gives command to spring the trap door after he has counted three. To give Bernard a chance to weaken he puts one minute between each count.

BELTON (CONT'D)
One----

A minute.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Two----

A minute.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Three----

The floor gives way.

BERNARD'S FALL

He shoots down with terrific speed. He nerves himself for the shock that is to tear his limbs from his body, but he feels the speed lessening as he falls, and his feet strike a floor with not sufficient force to even jar him severely.

BERNARD
(within himself)
Was this death? Was he dead or alive?

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - FIRST FLOOR - DAY

The mask is snatched from his face. He stands on a platform directly facing a large room containing desks arranged in a semi-circular form. One hundred and forty-five desks; at each a person seated. A parachute, arranged unknown to him, has saved him in the descent.

Within a few minutes Belton enters. The assemblage breaks forth into cheers. He walks to Bernard's side and spreads out his hands.

BELTON
Behold the Chiefs of the conspirators whom you would not betray. Behold me, whom they have called the arch conspirator. You have nobly stood the test. Come, your reward awaits you. You are worthy of it and I assure you it is worthy of you.

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - AISLE - DAY

Belton, smiling, locks his arm in Bernard's.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Come with me. I will explain it all to you.

They walk down the aisle together. The audience cheers wildly and a band of musicians begins playing "Hail to the Chief."

EXT. JEFFERSON COLLEGE - CAMPUS - DAY

Belton escorts him across the campus to the small but remarkably pretty white cottage, green vines clinging to trellis work all around it.

INT. THE WHITE COTTAGE - SITTING ROOM - DAY

Rare and antique furniture, tastefully arranged. A side room overlooking a beautiful lawn with lovely flowers and rose bushes. They sit facing each other.

BELTON
Bernard, I am now about to put the keeping of the property, the liberty, and the very lives of over seven million five hundred thousand people into your hands. Realize that I am carefully weighing each remark I make and am fully conscious of how much my statement involves.

Bernard bows his head in solemn thought. Viola's recent death, the blood-curdling experiences of the day, and Belton's impressive words make this as sober a moment as any he has had.

BERNARD
May revengeful lightning transfix me with her fiercest bolts; may hell's most fiery pillars roll in fury around me; may I be despised of man and forgotten of my God, if I ever knowingly, in the slightest way, do aught to betray this solemn, this most sacred trust.

Belton gazes fondly on his noble friend and sighs to think that only the coloring of his skin prevents his being enrolled upon the scroll of the very noblest sons of earth.

BELTON
I must begin. Another government, complete in every detail, exercising the sovereign right of life and death over its subjects, has been organized and maintained within the United States for many years. This government has a population of seven million two hundred and fifty thousand.

BERNARD
Do you mean all that you say, Belton?

BELTON
I shall in a short time submit to you positive proofs of my assertion. You shall find that I have not overstated anything.

BERNARD
But, Belton, how in the world can such a thing be when I, who am thoroughly conversant with every movement of any consequence, have not even dreamed of such a thing?

BELTON
All of that shall be made perfectly clear to you in the course of the narrative which I shall now relate.

INT. THE WHITE COTTAGE - EVENING

BELTON (CONT'D)
You will remember, Bernard, that there lived, in the early days of the American Republic, a negro scientist who won an international reputation by his skill and erudition. Because of his learning, this negro enjoyed the association of the moving spirits of the revolutionary period. By the publication of a book of science which outranked any other book of the day that treated of the same subject, he became a very wealthy man. This wealthy negro secretly gathered other free negroes together and organized a society that had a two-fold object. The first object was to secure for the free negroes all the rights and privileges of men, according to the teachings of Thomas Jefferson. Its other object was to secure the freedom of the enslaved negroes the world over.

BELTON (CONT'D)
When the War of Secession was over and all negroes were free, the society began cautiously to spread its membership among the emancipated. They conducted a campaign of education. He was taught that being released from chains was but the lowest form of liberty, and that he was no more than a common cur if he was satisfied with simply that. They made use of Jefferson's writings. This society used its influence secretly to have appointed over Southern schools of all kinds for negroes such teachers as would take especial pains to teach the negro to aspire for equality with all other races of men.

BELTON (CONT'D)
The negroes gained political ascendancy in many Southern states, but were soon hurled from power, by force in some quarters and by fraud in others. The federal government said: "Take care of yourselves, we are powerless to help you." The "Civil Rights Bill" was declared null and void by the Supreme Court. An "honest election bill" was defeated in Congress by James G. Blaine and others. Separate coach laws were declared constitutional. More than sixty thousand defenseless negroes were unlawfully slain.

BELTON (CONT'D)
One member of this society conceived the idea of making use of all of the secret orders already formed by negroes. These secret orders were all approached and asked to add one more degree and let this added degree be the same in every negro society. Each order remained, save in this last degree where all were one. This last degree was nothing more nor less than a compact government exercising all the functions of a nation.

BELTON (CONT'D)
All differences between the race were to be settled by this Government, as it had a well organized judiciary. An army was organized and every able-bodied citizen enlisted. A Congress was duly elected, one member for every fifty thousand citizens. Branch legislatures were formed in each state. There is only one branch to our Congress, the members of which are elected by a majority vote, for an indefinite length of time, and may be recalled at any time by a majority vote. Whenever a bill is introduced in the Congress of the United States affecting our race it is also introduced and debated here. Every race question submitted to the United States judiciary is also submitted to our own. A record of our decisions is kept side by side with the decisions of the United States.

BELTON (CONT'D)
The money which the scientist left was wisely invested, and at the conclusion of the civil war amounted to many millions. Good land at the South was offered after the war for twenty-five cents an acre. These millions were expended in the purchase of such lands, and our treasury is now good for $500,000,000. Our citizens own about $350,000,000. And all of this is pledged to our government in case it is needed. We have at our disposal, therefore, $850,000,000.

BELTON (CONT'D)
This place is known as Jefferson College, but it is in reality the Capitol of our Government, and those whom you have just left are the Congressmen.

BERNARD
But, Belton, how does it happen that I have been excluded from all this?

BELTON
The relation of your mother to the Anglo-Saxon race has not been clearly understood, and you and she have been under surveillance for many years. It was not until recently deemed advisable to let you in, your loyalty to the race never having fully been tested. I have been a member for years. While I was at Stowe University, though a young man, I was chairman of the bureau of education. While I was at Cadeville, La., that was my work. After my famous case I was elected to Congress here and soon thereafter chosen speaker, which position I now hold.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Our constitution expressly stipulates that the first President of our Government should be a man whom the people unanimously desired. In other words, we needed a George Washington. Various men have been put forward and all failed of the necessary unanimous vote. At last, one young man arose, who was brilliant and sound, genial and true, great and good. Unsolicited by him, unknown to him, the nation by its unanimous voice has chosen him the President of our beloved Government. This day he has unflinchingly met the test that our Congress decreed and has come out of the furnace, purer than gold. I have the great honor, on this the proudest occasion of my life, to announce that I am commissioned to inform you that the name of our President is Bernard Belgrave. You, sir, are President of the Imperium In Imperio, the name of our Government, and to you we devote our property, our lives, our all, promising to follow your banner into every post of danger until it is planted on freedom's hill. You are given three months in which to verify all of my claims, and give us answer as to whether you will serve us.

INT. THE IMPERIUM - THREE MONTHS (MONTAGE)

Bernard examines the reality and stability of the Imperium. He finds it well nigh perfect in every part.

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - DAY

Bernard assumes the Presidency and is duly inaugurated. He devotes much time to assuaging jealousies and healing breaches. He is so gentle, so loving, yet so firm and impartial, that all factional differences disappear at his approach.

INT. BERNARD'S OFFICE - JEFFERSON COLLEGE - NIGHT

He starts a secret newspaper to chronicle every fresh discrimination, every new act of oppression. An illustrated journal; pictures of horrors, commented upon in burning words, spread fire-brands everywhere in the ranks of the Imperium. Only members have access.

EXT. CUBA - NIGHT

An insurrection. The whole Imperium watches with keenest interest, the Cubans being in a large measure negroes. The United States sends a war ship. One night while the sailors sleep, some powerful engine of destruction demolishes the vessel and ends the lives of some 266 American seamen.

SUPER: "A BOARD OF INQUIRY DECIDES THE EXPLOSION WAS NOT INTERNAL AND ACCIDENTAL BUT EXTERNAL AND BY DESIGN. WAR BETWEEN THE UNITED STATES AND SPAIN IS PRACTICALLY INEVITABLE."

EXT. LAKE CITY, SOUTH CAROLINA - NIGHT

President McKinley has appointed Mr. [[Felix A. Cook]], a colored man of ability, culture and refinement, postmaster of Lake City, a village of 300 inhabitants. The white citizens made no protest.

A mob of white demons in human form assaults his home at night. They set fire to the house while the occupants sleep. When Cook and his family try to escape the flames, he is riddled with bullets and killed; his wife and children are wounded. The sole offense: that he accepted the honor which the government conferred.

INT. BERNARD'S OFFICE - DAY

Bernard regards this as the most opportune moment for the Imperium to meet and act upon the whole question of the relationship of the negro race to the Anglo-Saxons. The Congress of the Imperium is called in special session at the Capitol outside of Waco.

CUT TO:
